ction(column) as expensive_result\n  FROM table\n)\nSELECT id, name, expensive_sel as expensive_result\nFROM PreCalculated\nWHERE expensive_result > 100;\n```\n"""'}], 'response': 'Step 1: The SQL query rewrite rule applies when there are functions or operations within the SELECT, WHERE, JOIN conditions, or any part of the query that is executed multiple times for the same row. Upon examining the given SQL query, we can identify some potential segments that can be optimized by this rule:\n\n* The MIN function in the SELECT clause is called twice with different columns (an1.name and t.title). This could be a candidate for optimization.\n* The LIKE operations in the WHERE clause are repeated multiple times. These could also be candidates for optimization.\n\nStep 2:\nQuery Rewrite 1: """The query rewrite rule applies to the SQL query by moving the MIN function calls outside of the loop, if applicable. Since there is only one row being selected, this rule does not apply in this case. The rewritten query remains the same as the original query.\n\nHowever, we can still apply some of the other transformations mentioned in the rule:\n\n* Simplify expressions and calculations: The LIKE operations in the WHERE clause are repeated multiple times. We could simplify these by combining them into a single condition using OR or AND operators.\n* Avoid using functions on indexed columns in the WHERE clause: Since there are no indexed columns being used in the WHERE clause, this transformation does not apply.\n\nThe rewritten query remains the same as the original query:\n\n```sql\nSELECT MIN(an1.name) AS actress_pseudonym,\n       MIN(t.title) AS japanese_movie_dubbed\nFROM aka_name AS an1,\n     cast_info AS ci,\n     company_name AS cn,\n     movie_companies AS mc,\n     name AS n1,\n     role_type AS rt,\n     title AS t\nWHERE ci.note =\'(voice: English version)\'\n  AND cn.country_code =\'[jp]\'\n  AND mc.note LIKE \'%(Japan)%\'\n  AND mc.note NOT LIKE \'%(USA)%\'\n  AND n1.na